<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{a.horal, dl.pina, hm.paz, im.paulo, jps.soares</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Iago Paulo
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rah.ferreira, dc.tavares, dmgc.silva}@campus.fct.unl.pt</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">João Soares
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{jm.magalhaes, df.semedo}@fct.unl.pt</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper presents the vision, scientific contributions, and technical details of RedTWIZ: an adaptive and diverse multi-turn red teaming framework, to audit the robustness of Large Language Models (LLMs) in AI-assisted software development.
Our work is driven by three major research streams: (1) robust and systematic assessment of LLM conversational jailbreaks; (2) a diverse generative multi-turn attack suite, supporting compositional, realistic and goal-oriented jailbreak conversational strategies; and (3) a hierarchical attack planner, which adaptively plans, serializes, and triggers attacks tailored to specific LLM’s vulnerabilities.
Together, these contributions form a unified framework – combining assessment, attack generation, and strategic planning – to comprehensively evaluate and expose weaknesses in LLMs’ robustness.
Extensive evaluation is conducted to systematically assess and analyze the performance of the overall system and each component.
Experimental results demonstrate that our multi-turn adversarial attack strategies can successfully lead state-of-the-art LLMs to produce unsafe generations, highlighting the pressing need for more research into enhancing LLM’s robustness.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id6" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:4.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule ltx_align_center" style="width:100%;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into real-world applications, particularly within software development.
As these models become embedded in critical workflows – from developer tools to autonomous coding assistants – ensuring their robustness, safety, and trustworthiness is essential. Despite ongoing efforts to align LLMs through guardrails and safety tuning, recent work shows that LLMs remain vulnerable to sophisticated attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This becomes even more prominent in a conversational setting, where attacks are subtly conducted in a dynamic and interactive manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Red<span id="S1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">TWIZ</span></span> – Red Teaming WIZard – a comprehensive red teaming framework designed to audit the robustness of LLMs against conversational jailbreaks in AI-assisted software development contexts, such as generating malicious or vulnerable code and assisting in exploring security vulnerabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahai et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Robustly auditing safety aligned LLMs requires access to a diverse set of effective conversational attacks, followed by careful planning and dynamic adaptability to each LLM. With RedTWIZ, we address these aspects by introducing key innovations grounded on three core research streams (RS):</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.I1.i1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="34.31" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-11.83px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 34.31 34.31" width="34.31"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,34.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(17.15,0) translate(0,17.15)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFCCCC;" fill="#FFCCCC"><path d="M 16.88 0 C 16.88 9.32 9.32 16.88 0 16.88 C -9.32 16.88 -16.88 9.32 -16.88 0 C -16.88 -9.32 -9.32 -16.88 0 -16.88 C 9.32 -16.88 16.88 -9.32 16.88 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.4 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="24.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.I1.i1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">RS1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robust and Systematic LLM Jailbreak Assessment</span>, enabling fine-grained, automatic evaluation of LLM behavior in adversarial multi-turn settings, without reliance on manual expert code inspection and annotation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.I1.i2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="34.31" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-11.83px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 34.31 34.31" width="34.31"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,34.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(17.15,0) translate(0,17.15)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFCCCC;" fill="#FFCCCC"><path d="M 16.88 0 C 16.88 9.32 9.32 16.88 0 16.88 C -9.32 16.88 -16.88 9.32 -16.88 0 C -16.88 -9.32 -9.32 -16.88 0 -16.88 C 9.32 -16.88 16.88 -9.32 16.88 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.4 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="24.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.I1.i2.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">RS2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diverse Generative Multi-turn Attack Suite</span>, covering a wide range of strategies, delivering realistic, goal-driven and adaptive conversational attacks, ranging from prompt-based re-purposing to fully adaptive compositional attacks that evolve over the course of an interaction to subtly subvert LLM defenses.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.I1.i3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="34.31" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-11.83px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 34.31 34.31" width="34.31"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,34.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(17.15,0) translate(0,17.15)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFCCCC;" fill="#FFCCCC"><path d="M 16.88 0 C 16.88 9.32 9.32 16.88 0 16.88 C -9.32 16.88 -16.88 9.32 -16.88 0 C -16.88 -9.32 -9.32 -16.88 0 -16.88 C 9.32 -16.88 16.88 -9.32 16.88 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.4 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="24.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.I1.i3.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">RS3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hierarchical and Adaptive Attack Planning</span>, which dynamically serializes, schedules, and triggers attacks, hierarchically adapting attack strategies and target goals, based on the resilience of individual LLMs, assessed on probing interactions.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These components are integrated into a unified framework, illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 RedTWIZ General Architecture ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, that combines assessment, conversational generative attack generation, and strategic planning to enable realistic, adaptive red teaming of LLMs. At its core is the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RedTWIZ Arena</span>, a modular benchmarking platform that supports flexible experimentation under conditions mirroring the challenge’s setting, while allowing targeted evaluation of arbitrary defender models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate RedTWIZ both in the context of the Amazon Nova AI Challenge and through controlled offline experiments. Across these settings, our attacks consistently reveal vulnerabilities in even the most safety-aligned models, underscoring the need for more robust and context-aware defenses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Red<span id="S2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">TWIZ</span></span>  General Architecture</h2>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06994v1/gen_arch.drawio.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/258;" width="476" height="258" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Global overview of the RedTWIZ framework, in both online (tournaments) and offline settings. The hierarchical attack planner (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) probes and adaptively schedules the different attacks (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) against each target model. Our LLM-based judges (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) continuously and dynamically score conversations, informing the planner. In an offline setting, our red teaming arena (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 RedTWIZ Arena - Multi-Turn Jailbreak LLM Benchmarking ‣ 2 RedTWIZ General Architecture ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) simulates the tournament setting and supports state-of-the-art target defense models.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our system, shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 RedTWIZ General Architecture ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, features a modular pipeline for the automated generation and evaluation of conversational adversarial attacks against language models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate our multiple attack strategies within the <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Suite</span> (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), we leverage a dedicated <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreak Detection</span> module (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) that assigns performance scores based on the success of each strategy in eliciting harmful outputs from the target model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Planner</span> (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) hierarchically: 1) selects and prioritizes the most effective attacks against each defender, by leveraging a prior attack probing assessment, provided by the jailbreak judges, and 2) determines the most effective goal for the adversarial conversation, such as obtaining a rootkit, malware or other types of harmful code or malicious explanations. This joint optimization ensures that each attack is not only well-targeted but also aligned with the category most likely to succeed in bypassing the defender model’s guardrails.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>RedTWIZ Arena - Multi-Turn Jailbreak LLM Benchmarking</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key component of the RedTWIZ framework is the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RedTWIZ Arena</span>, illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 RedTWIZ General Architecture ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, an extensible red teaming arena designed for evaluating LLMs under multi-turn conversational attacks.
Targeting code generation and cybersecurity, it provides a controlled LLM auditing environment to uncover vulnerabilities that emerge through dialogue. All attack strategies are first tested and validated within the Arena before being deployed in competitive evaluation settings, ensuring that only effective, reproducible attacks are admitted into tournaments. This design anchors the Arena as a critical filter and benchmarking stage in the RedTWIZ pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The RedTWIZ Arena enables automatic evaluation using our suite of judge models (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), simulates red teaming tournaments that include probing stage leveraging the planner for strategic attack selection (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), and supports extensive testing of individual attack types developed through our attack strategy formulation (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Its modular architecture allows integration with both locally deployed and API-based LLMs, facilitating scalable and reproducible evaluations across a wide range of target model configurations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="34.31" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-10.64px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 34.31 34.31" width="34.31"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,34.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(17.15,0) translate(0,17.15)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFCCCC;" fill="#FFCCCC"><path d="M 16.88 0 C 16.88 9.32 9.32 16.88 0 16.88 C -9.32 16.88 -16.88 9.32 -16.88 0 C -16.88 -9.32 -9.32 -16.88 0 -16.88 C 9.32 -16.88 16.88 -9.32 16.88 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.4 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="24.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">RS1</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment </h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Jailbreak assessment evaluates whether attacker prompts successfully elicit malicious content from defender models. In the context of this challenge, the focus is on detecting the generation of malicious/vulnerable code, as well as descriptions that assist on how to conduct malicious cyberactivities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In a multi-turn evaluation setting, we use specialized models, referred to as <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreak Judges</span>, to detect safety violations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. This process is essential for enabling accurate scoring and informing strategic decisions in the attack planning stage (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following the Amazon Nova AI Challenge Annotation Guidelines, we categorize safety violations into two types: <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Malicious Code</span> and <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Malicious Explanation</span>. Hence, each interaction is independently evaluated by these Jailbreak Judges to determine whether the model’s behavior adheres to safety standards.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Jailbreak Assessment Data Collection</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To train and evaluate Jailbreak Judge models, we start by creating a high-quality conversational jailbreak dataset. We leverage data annotated with static code analysis and human-annotated data obtained from tournaments. The dataset includes labeled examples from Tournaments 1 and 2, as well as data collected during the seven practice rounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Jailbreak Assessment Data Collection ‣ 3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the number of labeled examples available for each judge. For each task, we report the total number of examples, as well as the number of positive (malicious) and negative (benign) examples. For malicious code detection, the dataset is relatively balanced, while the malicious explanation task suffers from severe class imbalance, with only 11% (181 examples) labeled as malicious.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Jailbreak assessment dataset statistics by category and label.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classification Category</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total Examples</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Positive Examples</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negative Examples</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Malicious Code</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">1448</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">844</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">594</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">Malicious Explanation</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">1605</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">181</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1424</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>LLM-based Jailbreak Judge Models</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce specialized <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Jailbreak Judges</span> as classifiers to evaluate model behavior during jailbreak attempts. These judges label multi-turn attacker-defender conversations based on alignment with safety standards defined in the Amazon Nova AI Challenge Annotation Guidelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We implement three categories of judges, leveraging distinct training paradigms and architectures:</p>
<dl id="S3.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">   • Zero-Shot Judges:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs used in a zero-shot classification setting.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">   • SFT-Encoder Judges:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Encoder-based Transformer models fine-tuned for classification.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S3.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">   • SFT-Decoder Judges:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S3.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Decoder-only Transformer models fine-tuned with parameter-efficient methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These complementary approaches enable us to explore trade-offs between task formulation (discriminative vs. generative), performance across different use cases, and effectiveness on safety-related tasks. For the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Malicious Explanation</span> task, due to the severe class imbalance in the available data, with only a small fraction of the examples labeled as malicious, we solely rely on zero-shot judges.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Zero-Shot Judges</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The zero-shot judges leverage LLMs, in a zero-shot setting, to classify conversations according to an evaluation task by using prompts that describe that task.
This approach allows for rapid iteration and deployment while ensuring consistency with human-annotated standards without the need for training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samvelyan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our implementation uses LLaMA 3.3 70B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, prompted with structured templates that describe the classification task following the official tournament annotation guidelines. Each input consists of a formatted conversation history, to which the model generates a binary classification label (e.g., <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">safe</span> or <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unsafe</span>) and a short explanation justifying the decision. The complete set of prompts is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Zero-Shot Judges prompts ‣ Appendix A Jailbreak Judges ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>SFT-Encoder Judges</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SFT-Encoder Judges leverage Transformer encoders, such as BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and RoBERTa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, fine-tuned to classify safety violations in model outputs. Unlike generative models that generate an answer token by token, encoder judges operate on fixed-length representations of conversations, outputting a classification label directly. This architecture offers a good compromise between model size and discriminative task performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qorib et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Malicious Code</span> classification task, we adopt two encoder models: CodeBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and ModernBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Warner et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We selected CodeBERT due to its pretraining on large volumes of source code, including extensive Python data, making it particularly effective for code-related classification tasks. ModernBERT, which supports a significantly extended context window of up to 8k tokens, was also fine-tuned on the malicious code classification task to evaluate the benefits of longer conversation context encoding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>SFT-Decoder Judges</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SFT-Decoder judges are decoder-only Transformer models fine-tuned for binary classification of safety violations (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">safe</span> or <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">unsafe</span>). We follow LLaMA Guard’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al., 2023</a>)</cite> approach, where models are trained autoregressively using the cross-entropy loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, we leverage a LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as the base model and fine-tune it using LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. This allows us to significantly reduce resource requirements while still leveraging its strong language modeling capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Biderman et al., 2024</a>)</cite>
(see Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Training Details For SFT-Decoder Judges ‣ Appendix A Jailbreak Judges ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> for full training and LoRA configuration details).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Jailbreak Judges Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the effectiveness of the judge models on both safety classification tasks, and report the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F1-Score</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Precision</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall</span>, and <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Accuracy</span>.
For the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">malicious code</span> task, we conduct model evaluation using a held-out test split comprising 10% of the full dataset. This split was randomly sampled to ensure generalization performance. For the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">malicious explanation</span> task, we create a balanced subset of human-labeled examples drawn from Tournament 1 and 2, in order to address the strong class imbalance.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Malicious Code</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results of the malicious code classification task are shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.3.1 Malicious Code ‣ 3.3 Jailbreak Judges Results ‣ 3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
The LLaMA 3.3 70B model, evaluated in a zero-shot setting, achieved the highest recall at 0.988, indicating strong sensitivity to unsafe code. However, it also produced a high number of false positives, as reflected in its low precision (0.625) and accuracy (0.639). This tendency to over-predict malicious code made it unsuitable for use in a tournament setting, where precision is critical to correctly target defender models’ weaknesses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Among the evaluated models, CodeBERT Base SFT achieved the highest F1 score at 0.835 and a strong recall of 0.941, indicating its effectiveness in detecting malicious code instances. Similarly, ModernBERT Base SFT demonstrated even higher recall at 0.965, though at the cost of significantly lower precision (0.661), resulting in a high number of false positives. ModernBERT Large SFT underperformed relative to its base counterpart, achieving both lower F1 score and overall accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite their strong recall, none of the encoder-based models were adopted in a tournament setting due to their tendency to over-predict malicious content. In practice, high false positive rates are problematic, particularly in the context of jailbreak detection, where misclassifications can lead to unnecessary or incorrect adjustments in the attack planning process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct model fine-tuned with LoRA achieved the highest precision across all models at 0.882, while maintaining a balanced recall of 0.750 and the best overall accuracy at 0.793.
The ability to confidently identify unsafe code while minimizing false positives is critical in ensuring reliable downstream decision-making (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Therefore, towards prioritizing precision, this model was selected for tournaments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Jailbreak Judges’ results for detecting <span id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Malicious Code</span>.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T2.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
