</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Malicious Explanation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results for the malicious explanation classification task are presented in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.3.2 Malicious Explanation ‣ 3.3 Jailbreak Judges Results ‣ 3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Due to the limited number of positive training examples, all models were evaluated in a zero-shot setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Among the evaluated models, LLaMA 3.3 70B Instruct achieved the best overall performance, with a precision of 0.846, recall of 0.669, F1 score of 0.747, and accuracy of 0.773. This reflects its strong ability to avoid false positives while still detecting a meaningful portion of malicious outputs. In contrast, Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2, despite achieving the highest precision at 0.860, missed a considerable number of malicious examples, as indicated by its low recall at 0.271. Mixtral 8x7B, on the other hand, detected more malicious outputs with a recall of 0.768, but at the cost of generating more false positives, as seen in its lower precision at 0.675.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As in the code classification task, the ability to confidently flag malicious explanations while minimizing false positives is crucial for supporting downstream decision-making (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
Therefore, given the need to prioritize precision and comply to the use of open-source models, LLaMA 3.3 70B was selected as the model for tournaments use in this task.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Jailbreak Judges’ results for detecting <span id="S3.T3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Malicious Explanations</span>.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T3.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T3.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T3.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T3.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge Model</span></th>
