<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="34.31" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-10.64px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 34.31 34.31" width="34.31"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,34.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(17.15,0) translate(0,17.15)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFCCCC;" fill="#FFCCCC"><path d="M 16.88 0 C 16.88 9.32 9.32 16.88 0 16.88 C -9.32 16.88 -16.88 9.32 -16.88 0 C -16.88 -9.32 -9.32 -16.88 0 -16.88 C 9.32 -16.88 16.88 -9.32 16.88 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.4 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="24.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">RS2</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work on jailbreaking LLMs has introduced a range of adversarial prompting techniques, from single-turn attacks that obfuscate harmful intent using ciphers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lv et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or structured noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, to iterative refinement methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mehrotra et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Others rely on persuasive language strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> or automated search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>. Recent work has begun exploring multi-turn jailbreaking, where adversarial strategies unfold over multiple turns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, but remains relatively underexplored in the context of cybersecurity and code-oriented tasks. Our work builds on this line of research and takes a step further by designing structured multi-turn attack strategies that adapt to defender behavior and model-specific weaknesses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces the full set of attack strategies that we developed for the competition. Each strategy was designed to exploit different weaknesses of the defenders’ safety mechanisms, ranging from attacks that follow predefined prompt structures to adaptive conversations that evolve based on model responses. Although the underlying intent varies – from seeking insecure code completions to mimicking benign queries with adversarial objectives – all attacks were constructed to simulate realistic user interactions while subtly introducing harmful objectives. For illustration purposes only, examples of attacks and defender model responses are shown in Appendix <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Malicious Conversations Examples ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Utility Poisoning Attacks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by introducing a set of simple, yet effective attacks that leverage the challenge’s <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utility Dataset</span> – a collection of harmless prompts provided to defenders during system development to simulate safe user interactions. These attacks are based on a simple observation: when defenders encounter an innocuous user query, they are expected to respond. This initial cooperation sets a conversational precedent, making it harder to reject later queries that escalate malicious intent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The utility dataset naturally included both coding and security-related queries, and we preserved this distinction to structure two parallel attack strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Each attack was generated using an LLM acting as the attacker, guided by dedicated system prompts tailored to the intent of each utility-based strategy (see Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H System prompts ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a> for representative prompts). For each sample in the utility dataset, we generate a corresponding set of five conversational queries that escalate in maliciousness. These were composed in advance, without conditioning on the defender’s responses, to maintain alignment with the original prompt. By mimicking familiar request formats and gradually introducing adversarial pressure, the attacks subtly shifted the context toward harmful intent. Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> outlines the overall structure of the Utility Poisoning attack strategy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06994v1/coding_utility.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/111;" width="476" height="111"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Diagram of Utility Poisoning Attack Strategies. The five queries are generated upfront based on a seed utility prompt, without being conditioned on the online defender’s responses.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Utility Coding Prompt Exploit</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This strategy targets programming-related prompts in the utility dataset, which typically ask for functional code solutions to common development tasks. Rather than directly requesting harmful behaviors, the attacker begins with a standard request and progressively layers in additional functionality – such as retry logic, arbitrary query execution, and vulnerability scanning – each framed as a benign usability or debugging enhancement (see Appendix <a href="#A8.SS1" title="H.1 Utility Coding Prompt Exploit ‣ Appendix H System prompts ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This staged escalation exploits the coding assistant’s tendency to fulfill technically valid requests without questioning their broader intent. By adhering closely to the language, structure, and tone of the original utility prompts, the queries avoid triggering explicit safety mechanisms, yet still guide the model toward producing code that enables insecure behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Security Events Utility Exploit</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the coding-focused utility attack, this strategy targets the model’s natural language explanations. Rather than requesting code completions, the attacker crafts harmless-seeming questions about cybersecurity topics – such as system hardening, access control, or threat detection – to elicit insecure reasoning, unsafe recommendations, or descriptions of misconfigurations and bypass techniques.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This approach subtly shifts the attack surface from code generation to security-relevant language modeling, probing the model’s ability to maintain safety boundaries in explanatory contexts (see Appendix <a href="#A8.SS2" title="H.2 Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ Appendix H System prompts ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.2</span></a>). By mimicking the tone and structure of legitimate prompts, these queries remain difficult to flag as adversarial, despite their manipulative intent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              All experiments were conducted in a safe and controlled environment, where the model could not execute or act on any malicious generations. See Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Ethics and Responsible Disclosure ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> for more details.</span></span></span> shows that despite not being optimized for general-purpose LLMs, Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2 exhibited notable resistance to coding-related exploits, while Amazon Nova Pro showed relative strength against malicious explanation attacks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Attack success rates of the Utility Poisoning Attacks across three different LLMs. All attacks were generated using LLaMA 3.3 70B as the attacker model. All interactions were evaluated using our custom judges (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). <a href="#footnote1" title="Footnote 1 ‣ Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Type</span></th>
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct</th>
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2</th>
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Amazon Nova Pro</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Coding Utility Prompt Exploit</th>
<td id="S4.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">57.5%</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">31.5%</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">77.5%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">Security Events Utility Exploit</th>
<td id="S4.T4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">85.0%</td>
