<td id="S4.T7.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">D</td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">83.97</td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">45.04</td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">76.34</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T7.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T7.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">E</td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T7.2.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">89.10</span></td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T7.2.7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">50.71</span></td>
<td id="S4.T7.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T7.2.7.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">86.26</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>RedTreez</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RedTreez</span> is a black<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>box, tree<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>based adversarial framework that exploits LLMs through adaptive multi<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>turn conversations, with look-ahead attack strategies’ sequence optimization. By classifying model responses to guide subsequent attacks, it dynamically constructs a “Tree of Strategies” whose branches represent distinct attack-defense trajectories. We visualize this adaptive process in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.4.1 Motivation for a Tree Structure ‣ 4.4 RedTreez ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. For a formal overview of the full attack execution loop, see Algorithm <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C RedTreez Algorithm ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. In the following sections, we first motivate why dialogues are modeled as trees (Section <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS1" title="4.4.1 Motivation for a Tree Structure ‣ 4.4 RedTreez ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4.1</span></a>), then describe the attack stage (Section <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS2" title="4.4.2 Attack Module (A Nodes) ‣ 4.4 RedTreez ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4.2</span></a>), the response classification and judging mechanism (Section <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS3" title="4.4.3 Response Classification and Judging (D Nodes) ‣ 4.4 RedTreez ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4.3</span></a>), and finally the pruning and adaptation process that keeps the tree efficient to traverse (Section <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS4" title="4.4.4 Pruning and Adaptation ‣ 4.4 RedTreez ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.1 </span>Motivation for a Tree Structure</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-turn red teaming with LLMs naturally creates branches whenever a defender responds unpredictably. Modeling this as a tree allows RedTreez to capture this branching nature efficiently and maintain a diverse repertoire of high<span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>level strategies
rather than relying on a flat list of prompts. This is crucial because LLMs introduce inherent randomness via sampling temperature, so a strategy reused under different seeds and different target models can produce varied results. By structuring interactions as a tree, it becomes possible to track which strategy-response paths yield the highest jailbreak success, propagating these success metrics upward to prioritize effective branches. Additionally, this design allows pruning of low<span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>performance sub-trees, reallocating computational effort toward the most promising attack trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Another important design decision was to abstract defender responses into response labels instead of storing raw replies directly. This simplifies the tree structure while preserving flexibility, as it allows different malicious goals to share a consistent set of outcome labels even when surface-level responses differ. Although the system could be extended to track full responses if required, working with labels makes the framework more scalable and keeps the attack logic general-purpose without compromising efficacy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06994v1/redTreezOverview.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/148;" width="476" height="148"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>RedTreez Overview</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.2 </span>Attack Module (A Nodes)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RedTreez</span>, every conversation unfolds over a fixed sequence of five turns, corresponding to up to ten alternating <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> (Attacker) and <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span> (Defender) nodes in the tree. Even if a jailbreak is achieved before the final turn, the interaction continues to the end so that each stage can attempt to elicit ever more malicious outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> node represents an attacker’s decision point in the conversation tree and encapsulates a specific set of attack strategies to be used at a given turn. These strategies are either sampled from a predefined pool (Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.4.1 Motivation for a Tree Structure ‣ 4.4 RedTreez ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) or dynamically generated through strategy-guiding prompts, allowing them to incorporate the dialogue history for contextual meaning.
Each new <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> node contains its own combination of attack strategies – for example, <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[‘a story about a king’, ‘be assertive’]</span> – which are later incorporated into attack prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The attack module operates in one of three modes, each tailored to different objectives within the framework.
While these modes do not necessarily occur in a fixed sequence during regular tree traversal, they typically correspond to different phases of system operation.
In <em id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Population Mode</em>, used during the initial tree-building phase, the framework focuses on growing the tree by creating new <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> nodes whenever the current node has a number of children below a user-specified maximum size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Once the tree reaches a user-defined size, <em id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Coverage Mode</em> is employed to explore existing branches by selecting child <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> nodes at random. This aims to uncover unforeseen defender behaviors and diversify the repertoire of possible attack paths by spawning new branches wherever necessary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, in contexts where the system needs to leverage its accumulated knowledge for efficient exploitation – such as during tournament evaluation – the framework operates in <em id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Exploitation Mode</em>. Here, the <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> node with the highest recorded jailbreak success rate is selected at each step to guide prompt generation, steering interactions along the most historically effective paths.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Once an <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> node has been selected during traversal – whether in <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Population</span>, <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Coverage</span>, or <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Exploitation</span> mode – the corresponding attack prompt is generated by combining a turn-specific guiding prompt template with the current conversation context.
These guiding prompts, detailed in Appendix <a href="#A8.SS7" title="H.7 RedTreez ‣ Appendix H System prompts ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.7</span></a>, are pre-defined strings containing placeholders such as {goal}, {history}, and {extra_info}. The {extra_info} tag is dynamically populated with the list of strategies attached to the current <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> node, typically introduced via a sentence like <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Use these strategies: …”</span>. By concatenating the appropriate guiding prompt with the conversation history and node-specific strategies, <span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RedTreez</span> constructs a complete attack prompt tailored to both the current turn and attack path. This mechanism allows the attacker model to generate contextually informed jailbreak attempts while preserving turn-specific behavioral nuances.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.3 </span>Response Classification and Judging (D Nodes)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After each attack prompt is issued, it is sent to the target model along with the conversation history. The resulting response is abstracted into a set of predefined labels – refusing, complying, no code, code – using an external classifier model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span> node represents a defender decision point in the conversation tree and corresponds to a specific combination of these response labels under a given <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> node. Each <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span> node acts as a record of how the defender reacted to an attack attempt at that stage, and contains the label set it represents, along with associated metadata such as whether a jailbreak occurred at that point and how often the path has been exercised.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Within the scope of a given <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> node, identical label sets will always correspond to the same <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span> node. In other words, the tree allows multiple <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span> nodes with the same label combination to exist in different parts of the tree, but under any particular <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> node, each unique label combination is represented by a single <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span> node. This ensures consistency in how defender behaviors are captured along specific conversational paths while preventing redundant branches at the same decision point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Each time a <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span> node is reached during an interaction, it is registered as having been exercised. If the associated defender response meets the jailbreak criteria (using the judges discussed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), this outcome is recorded at that <span id="S4.SS4.SSS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">D</span> node and the success is also propagated upward to all ancestor nodes along the path – provided they have not already been credited for a jailbreak during the current conversation. This mechanism ensures that repeated jailbreaks within the same interaction only reinforce newly successful parts of the tree, while prior nodes maintain accurate, non-duplicated success records. In cases where no jailbreak occurs, only the activity of having traversed the node is noted. As a result, the success rate of each branch naturally adjusts over time, reflecting both its historical effectiveness and the frequency with which it has been challenged.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.4 </span>Pruning and Adaptation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To prevent unbounded growth, <span id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RedTreez</span> routinely removes branches whose cumulative success rate falls below a configurable threshold. By pruning these underperforming <span id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A–D</span> sub-trees, the system concentrates computational resources on the most promising attack paths. Should a previously pruned response pattern reappear during later coverage or population phases, a new branch is created under the appropriate <span id="S4.SS4.SSS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A</span> node, ensuring that the tree adapts continuously to evolving defender behaviors.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>RedTreez ASR (custom judges) using Deepseek V3 and a fine-tuned LLaMA 3.1 model. <a href="#footnote1" title="Footnote 1 ‣ Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></figcaption>
