</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.5 </span>RedTreez Results</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the results of eliciting unsafe completions using the <span id="S4.SS4.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RedTreez</span> framework. The underlying strategy tree was populated through multi<span id="S4.SS4.SSS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>turn interactions with Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2 as the defender model, ensuring a diverse set of attack-defense paths.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For this evaluation, we fine-tune a medium-sized language model, LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, using outputs collected from instances where DeepSeek V3 elicited unsafe completions in controlled adversarial benchmarks. The goal of this fine-tuning process was to adapt LLaMA 3.1 into a red-teaming attacker model, capable of generating prompts optimized to bypass safety mechanisms in target models. To create the training dataset, a series of interactions were carried out with DeepSeek V3 against various defender models. The resulting data – including ASR scores, conversation histories, and the specific strategies that led to these outcomes – were compiled and used to supervise the fine-tuning of LLaMA 3.1 for this attack-oriented task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.4.4 Pruning and Adaptation ‣ 4.4 RedTreez ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> reports ASR scores when using the original DeepSeek V3 attacker and the fine-tuned LLaMA 3.1 model, each tested against a range of defender models. Notably, even though the tree was built using Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2, the learned strategies transfer effectively to other architectures, yielding substantially higher success rates on the LLaMA series.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Red-DAT: Multi-turn Red-teaming with Dialogue Action Tokens</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In multi-turn red teaming scenarios, where attackers must strategically guide the conversation over several turns to bypass defenses, turn-by-turn adaptability is essential. To achieve this adaptability, we introduce <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Red-DAT</span>, an approach that steers attacker behavior through reinforcement-learning conversational attack planning, without modifying the underlying language model. Our method is inspired by the Dialogue Action Tokens (DAT) framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which demonstrated how a frozen LLM can be guided using a small trainable neural network, called a <span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Steering Policy</span>, that outputs control tokens. We extend this framework to adversarial, multi-turn settings, where the goal is to elicit unsafe completions in a controlled adversarial benchmark targeting a defender model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Steering policy</span> operates in a low-dimensional, continuous action space. At each turn, it observes the conversation history and produces trainable action vectors, used as prefix embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Liang, 2021</a>)</cite> that steer the frozen LLM’s generation. This design enables fine-grained control without modifying the model’s parameters, making the approach modular and broadly applicable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional approaches to dialogue steering often rely on discrete action spaces, where each “action” corresponds to a fixed set of tokens or a specific type of language behavior. However, this discrete space can limit the flexibility required for complex, dynamic, multi-turn dialogues.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.1 </span>Red-DAT Key Components</h4>

<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06994v1/red_dat.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/267;" width="476" height="267" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>
<span id="S4.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Red-DAT</span> architecture. In stage one, the <span id="S4.F6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Steering Policy</span> predicts a control vector based on the conversation history. In stage two, <span id="S4.F6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Controlled Attack Generation</span> , this vector guides the frozen attacker language model to generate the next utterance. A reward model evaluates the defender’s response and provides feedback to improve future actions.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attacker LLM:</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Attacker LLM</span> is a pretrained language model (we use LLaMA 3.1 8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) that remains frozen during both training and inference. It generates utterances conditioned on the conversation history and the control tokens produced by the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Steering Policy</span>, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.5.1 Red-DAT Key Components ‣ 4.5 Red-DAT: Multi-turn Red-teaming with Dialogue Action Tokens ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Steering Policy:</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Steering Policy</span> <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is a lightweight neural network that outputs a low-dimensional action vector <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the conversation history embedding <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This vector is mapped into the LLM’s embedding space using a learned matrix <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, producing control token embeddings (dialogue action tokens) that are prepended to the input sequence. These tokens steer the frozen LLM’s output without modifying its internal parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward Model:</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reward model</span> serves to evaluate how well the current state of the conversation aligns with the goal by providing feedback for each turn in the conversation.
In our setup, we use the HarmBench LLM Classifier <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as the reward model, assigning a continuous reward score between 0 and 1, where higher scores indicate a higher likelihood of generating unsafe completions.
We use HarmBench’s classifier as it offers a reliable and standardized approach to jailbreak detection, enabling consistent evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, we calculate the jailbreak reward score for a given turn as:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}=\frac{1}{1+e^{-(y_{\text{Yes}}-y_{\text{No}})/\tau}}\ ," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>Yes</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>No</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}=\frac{1}{1+e^{-(y_{\text{Yes}}-y_{\text{No}})/\tau}}\ ,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{Yes}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>Yes</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{Yes}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{No}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>No</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{No}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the logits assigned by the HarmBench judge model to classify if there was a jailbreak, and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature parameter (we used <math id="S4.SS5.SSS1.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>=10). This reward formulation provides a soft, continuous signal reflecting the degree of success at each turn.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.2 </span>Reinforcement Learning in Red-DAT</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Red-DAT relies on <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reinforcement Learning (RL)</span> to train the policy that steers the attacker’s behavior. The training process follows the two-stage procedure introduced in the Dialogue Action Tokens framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Self-Cloning Initialization</span> followed by the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RL Stage</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Self-Cloning Initialization:</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The policy is trained to reproduce the baseline attacker’s utterances given the dialogue history, effectively aligning its initial policy with the unsteered Attacker LLM behavior. This ensures that, before reward-driven optimization is introduced, the policy produces control signals that guide the model to generate fluent, coherent, and contextually grounded responses. To support this, we collect a dataset of 10,000 conversations using the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RedTWIZ Arena</span> (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 RedTWIZ Arena - Multi-Turn Jailbreak LLM Benchmarking ‣ 2 RedTWIZ General Architecture ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), each consisting of 5 turns to simulate tournament behavior. In these conversations, the attacker is a LLaMA 3.1-8B model guided by a system prompt, while the defenders used are LLaMA 3.1-8B-Instruct and Claude 3 Haiku, which serve as the target models during training data generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RL Stage:</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After self-cloning, the policy <math id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained using the offline reinforcement learning algorithm <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">TD3+BC</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fujimoto and Gu, 2021</a>)</cite>, which is well-suited for continuous action spaces. To build the training buffer, we generated an additional 10,000 exploratory conversations using the self-cloned policy with Gaussian noise <math id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,0.2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,0.2)</annotation></semantics></math> added in action space, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite>. All training was conducted against a static LLaMA 3.1-8B defender again using our simulation arena (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 RedTWIZ Arena - Multi-Turn Jailbreak LLM Benchmarking ‣ 2 RedTWIZ General Architecture ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>).
Moreover, to improve training stability, we adopt a residual architecture as described in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Reinforcement Learning Training Details ‣ Appendix D Red-DAT Implementation Details ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Red-DAT results with different defender models during training. <a href="#footnote1" title="Footnote 1 ‣ Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></figcaption>
