</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Results</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ RL Stage: ‣ 4.5.2 Reinforcement Learning in Red-DAT ‣ 4.5 Red-DAT: Multi-turn Red-teaming with Dialogue Action Tokens ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows the performance Red-DAT across multiple target models using the RedTwiz Judges Success Rate (ASR) metric, evaluated in our controlled red-teaming benchmark. The Unsteered column reflects the attack model without any steering policy applied. During training example generation, LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct and Claude 3 Haiku were used as target models. In the LLaMA only setting, only LLaMA was used; in Mixed Data, both LLaMA and Claude were used; and in Curriculum, the policy was trained on examples against LLaMA and then on examples against Claude. Overall, the Mixed Data demonstrated improved ability to elicit unsafe completions across target models in benchmark conditions, showing improved robustness and transferability over the original framework, where only LLaMA is used as a target model.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.3 </span>Improvements Over the Original DAT Framework</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance the effectiveness of Red-DAT, we introduced a key modification: an <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aggressiveness Penalty</span>, designed to discourage overly direct attack strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We applied this modification on top of our best setup, Mixed Data, which used both LLaMA 3.1 8B and Claude 3 Haiku as target models.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attack Aggressiveness Penalty:</h5>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This improvement focused on refining the reward function by introducing an <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aggressiveness Penalty</span>. Since Red-DAT is designed for multi-turn attacks, this modification aimed to discourage the attacker from becoming too aggressive in the early stages of the conversation. This is done, as prematurely aggressive prompts can trigger defensive responses from the target model, ultimately lowering the overall success rate of the attack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the aggressiveness of the attacker’s prompts, we evaluated their harmfulness using LLaMA Guard 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. As with the reward function, we computed the harmfulness score <math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at each turn <math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> using the logits <math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{unsafe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>unsafe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{unsafe}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the model:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}=\frac{1}{1+e^{-(y_{\text{unsafe}}-y_{\text{safe}})/\tau}}\ ," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>unsafe</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}=\frac{1}{1+e^{-(y_{\text{unsafe}}-y_{\text{safe}})/\tau}}\ ,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=10</annotation></semantics></math>, matching the temperature used in the jailbreak reward.
We calculated the reward penalty as:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}=\lambda\cdot\left(1-\frac{t-1}{T-1}\right)\cdot h_{t}\ ," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}=\lambda\cdot\left(1-\frac{t-1}{T-1}\right)\cdot h_{t}\ ,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum penalty weight, which we set to 0.25 based on small-scale tuning experiments, ensuring that the penalty contributed meaningfully without overwhelming the reward. Here, <math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is the current dialogue turn, and <math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of turns in the conversation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation applies the strongest penalty at the first turn (<math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>), linearly decreasing to zero at the final turn (<math id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T</annotation></semantics></math>).
The final penalized reward used for training was:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}=r_{t}-p_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}=r_{t}-p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The gradual reduction of the penalty weight ensured that the escalation process was controlled, ultimately improving the elicitation rate of unsafe completions in red-teaming benchmarks, as it can be seen in Table <a href="#S4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Attack Aggressiveness Penalty: ‣ 4.5.3 Improvements Over the Original DAT Framework ‣ 4.5 Red-DAT: Multi-turn Red-teaming with Dialogue Action Tokens ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T10" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>Red-DAT results with Aggressiveness Penalty. <a href="#footnote1" title="Footnote 1 ‣ Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T10.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T10.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T10.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T10.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Model</span></th>
