</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.4 </span>Generalization to Unseen Malicious Goals</h4>

<figure id="S4.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>Red-Dat results with Unseen Malicious Goals. <a href="#footnote1" title="Footnote 1 ‣ Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T11.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T11.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T11.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T11.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Model</span></th>
<th id="S4.T11.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T11.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unsteered (Baseline)</span></th>
<th id="S4.T11.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T11.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Steering Policy</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T11.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T11.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct</th>
<td id="S4.T11.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T11.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text">76.25</span></td>
<td id="S4.T11.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T11.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">77.25</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T11.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T11.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Claude 3 Haiku</th>
<td id="S4.T11.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T11.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text">51.00</span></td>
<td id="S4.T11.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T11.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">57.25</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T11.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T11.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Claude 3.5 Sonnet</th>
<td id="S4.T11.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T11.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text">30.50</span></td>
<td id="S4.T11.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T11.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">33.50</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T11.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T11.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Amazon Nova Pro</th>
<td id="S4.T11.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T11.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text">66.25</span></td>
<td id="S4.T11.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T11.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">70.25</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the generalization capabilities of the trained steering policy, we tested it on a new dataset containing cybersecurity-related malicious goals that were not seen during training. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ 4.5.4 Generalization to Unseen Malicious Goals ‣ 4.5 Red-DAT: Multi-turn Red-teaming with Dialogue Action Tokens ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, applying the Steering Policy consistently improved performance across all target models compared to the unsteered baseline. These results demonstrate that the learned policy can adapt to new types of malicious objectives, highlighting its potential in simulated red-teaming where diverse and evolving attack goals are essential for thoroughly probing model vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="34.31" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-10.64px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 34.31 34.31" width="34.31"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,34.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(17.15,0) translate(0,17.15)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFCCCC;" fill="#FFCCCC"><path d="M 16.88 0 C 16.88 9.32 9.32 16.88 0 16.88 C -9.32 16.88 -16.88 9.32 -16.88 0 C -16.88 -9.32 -9.32 -16.88 0 -16.88 C 9.32 -16.88 16.88 -9.32 16.88 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.4 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="24.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">RS3</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span> Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior research by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al. (2024)</a></cite> has shown that the effectiveness of jailbreak strategies varies significantly across model families and sizes.
For instance, GCG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite> achieves 34.5% success on LLaMA 3.1 8B but drops to 17% on the larger LLaMA 3.3 70B, whereas PAIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2023</a>)</cite> exhibits the opposite trend, improving from 18.7% to 36%.
Other attacks, like PAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, demonstrate relatively stable success rates within a model family (e.g., 16% on both LLaMA models), but vary substantially across architectures, reaching 42% on GPT-4o and only 2% on Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2. These results highlight that different models exhibit distinct vulnerabilities to different attack strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While we lack explicit knowledge of the specific model architectures or training regimens used by the defenders, we know that all defenders started with the same base system and then evolved individually using distinct strategies. As a result, these defenders developed different capabilities and weaknesses, which our <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner</span> aims to identify and exploit.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Optimizing Attack Strategy Allocation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the beginning of the competition, there was significant uncertainty regarding each defender’s weaknesses and the relative effectiveness of different attack strategies. Adding to this challenge, attacker teams have a limited interaction budget, making every decision critical to maximize success.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, we framed the attack selection process as a <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multi-armed bandit (MAB)</span> problem, where each of our attack strategies was treated as an arm and success feedback served as the reward. This formulation allowed us to balance <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exploration</span>, by testing diverse attacks to uncover potential weaknesses, and <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exploitation</span>, by prioritizing strategies with demonstrated success.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, our planner operates in two sequential phases: a <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probing Stage</span>, which allows attackers to explore and gather information about each defender’s behavior, followed by a <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tournament Stage</span>, where attacks contribute to the final ASR and insights gained during the probing phase can be leveraged to inform strategic decisions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This setup allowed us to first observe how different strategies performed before committing to a specific allocation policy during the tournament stage. To implement this framework in practice, we developed four planner algorithms, each offering different exploration-exploitation trade-offs:</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Round Robin</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Served as our baseline planner. Cycles uniformly through the available attack strategies, assigning equal opportunity to each without regard to past success or failure. This approach ensured unbiased exploration across all strategies but lacks adaptability to defender weaknesses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Epsilon-Greedy</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prioritizes exploiting the highest-performing attack strategy while maintaining a probability <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> of selecting a random alternative. This approach allows the planner to focus on successful strategies while continuing to explore potentially better options, preventing premature convergence on suboptimal choices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Auer, 2002</a>)</cite></h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Selects attack strategies by maximizing an upper-bound estimate of their success potential, combining empirical performance with an exploration bonus favoring less-tested strategies. We used the standard formula:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UCB}_{i}=\hat{\mu}_{i}+c\sqrt{\frac{\ln n}{n_{i}}}\ ," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>UCB</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UCB}_{i}=\hat{\mu}_{i}+c\sqrt{\frac{\ln n}{n_{i}}}\ ,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the observed success rate of strategy <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of trials, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of times strategy <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> was selected, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a tunable exploration parameter. UCB dynamically balances exploration and exploitation without requiring manual exploration rates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Thompson Sampling</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Maintains a posterior distribution over each strategy’s success rate, initialized with a uniform Beta(1,1) prior. At each decision point, it samples from these distributions and selects the strategy with the highest sampled value. This approach naturally balances uncertainty and observed outcomes, adapting efficiently to changing defender weaknesses.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Attack Strategy Allocation Across Planners</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to select the most appropriate algorithm to use in the tournaments, we conducted several tests simulating realistic challenge conditions using the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RedTWIZ Arena</span> (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 RedTWIZ Arena - Multi-Turn Jailbreak LLM Benchmarking ‣ 2 RedTWIZ General Architecture ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>).
For each simulation, the Probing Stage used 200 interactions using the Round-Robin algorithm to ensure a fair starting point across all attack strategies, followed by 200 interactions in the Tournament Stage, where the planner selected the attack strategy. Table <a href="#S5.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ 5.2 Attack Strategy Allocation Across Planners ‣ 5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> presents the distribution of attacks and corresponding success rates across all four planner algorithms, with success evaluated using our Jailbreak Judges (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Naturally, all planner algorithms that incorporate feedback – Epsilon Greedy, UCB, and Thompson Sampling – outperform the baseline Round-Robin approach in terms of ASR, as they aim to optimize attack selection based on observed defender behavior. While the overall ASR of these three adaptive strategies are comparable, UCB stands out by maintaining a more balanced distribution across different attack types. This indicates that UCB continues to explore a broader range of strategies instead of prematurely concentrating on a narrow set of high-performing ones – an outcome that aligns with our goal of maintaining strategy diversity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T12" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span>Comparison of attack distribution and success rates across four planners on the target model LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct. All algorithms assume a 200-interaction Round-Robin Probing Stage followed by 200 interactions for each algorithm in the Tournament Stage. The columns show the total number of attacks, successes, and the ASR for each planner and attack strategy. All interactions were evaluated using our Jailbreak Judges (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). <a href="#footnote1" title="Footnote 1 ‣ Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T12.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:60.7pt;vertical-align:-29.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-173.2pt,30.5pt) scale(0.498957691129002,0.498957691129002) ;">
