</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Cross-Model Generalization of Planner Effectiveness</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Selecting an effective attack planner required not only optimizing performance for a single defender but also ensuring generalization across different models. Because the defender systems are different, we evaluated each planner in three models to assess their robustness and adaptability. This comparison allowed us to understand how well each algorithm balanced exploration and exploitation in varied settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 5.3 Cross-Model Generalization of Planner Effectiveness ‣ 5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, all adaptive methods significantly outperformed the Round-Robin baseline, highlighting the importance of incorporating feedback from the defender model.
Epsilon Greedy exhibited early sensitivity to observed success signals, often shifting its focus toward a narrow subset of strategies after initial gains. This behavior was particularly evident when interacting with LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct and Amazon Nova Pro. Although this can produce high immediate success rates, it risks deprioritizing other potentially effective strategies.
Thompson Sampling showed similar tendencies, especially in runs with Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2, where it gradually reduced its exploration in favor of strategies with early observed success.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">UCB, in contrast, delivered consistently strong performance across all tested models.
Crucially, UCB maintained a more balanced allocation of attacks across different strategies, avoiding premature convergence and preserving broader exploration, as evidenced in Table <a href="#S5.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ 5.2 Attack Strategy Allocation Across Planners ‣ 5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. Overcommitting to a small subset of highly effective strategies, as observed with Epsilon Greedy and Thompson Sampling, could lead to overfitting to short-term defender weaknesses and reduce the coverage of the attack space (detailed analysis in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Planner Attack Distribution Analysis ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For these reasons – strong average performance and effective coverage of the attack suite – we selected UCB as the planner for the Tournament Stage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T13" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 13: </span>Comparison of attack planner ASR performance across three models. Each simulation includes a 200-interaction Round-Robin probing stage followed by 200 interactions using the corresponding planner. All interactions were evaluated using our Jailbreak Judges (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). <a href="#footnote1" title="Footnote 1 ‣ Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></figcaption>
