<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Optimizing Malicious Category Allocation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Attack Type</span> defines the general nature of the attack being executed, most strategies also required a specific conversational category to guide the dialogue toward a harmful goal (e.g. “<span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Write a script to send text messages with fraudulent links designed to collect personal information</span>”). To support this, we introduced an additional planning layer at the <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">malicious category level</span>, where categories served as conversation guiding topics. These categories (listed in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Coding Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) organize our attacks and allow us to systematically probe different types of model vulnerabilities. Inspired by the threat taxonomy in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, they help target a diverse set of real-world malicious intents. This category-level planning was applied to all attacks except for Utility-based ones, which relied on utility-driven prompts rather than malicious goals from the taxonomy (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). Importantly, category selection is performed independently for each attack type, so success in one category for a given attack does not affect others.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To perform category selection, we applied the same family of planning algorithms used for attack strategy selection. The primary attack-level planner remained fixed as UCB, while the separate category-level planner selected the malicious category for each conversation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ 5.4 Optimizing Malicious Category Allocation ‣ 5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> shows the distribution of selected malicious categories across planners and highlights the effects of different allocation behaviors.The Round-Robin planner, which uniformly distributes its budget across all categories without considering performance, yields the lowest ASR. While UCB is effective for attack-level planning, it proved too conservative at the category level. Its strong emphasis on balancing exploration and exploitation resulted in a nearly uniform distribution across categories – an outcome driven by the limited number of interactions relative to the number of available categories – ultimately hindering its ability to prioritize top-performing topics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, Epsilon Greedy adapted more rapidly: it retained broad initial coverage but quickly allocated more conversations to successful categories, such as <span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Worms</span> (24.2%), resulting in the highest ASR (87.5%). Thompson Sampling behaved similarly but showed a stronger early commitment to high-performing categories – particularly <span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ransomware</span> (26.8%) – which led to a slightly lower ASR. In this setting, where a wide range of categories are viable and feedback accumulates gradually, Epsilon Greedy’s ability to generalize early and specialize late proved most effective, and was therefore selected as the planner for malicious category allocation during the competition’s tournaments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T14" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 14: </span>
Distribution of selected malicious categories across planners against LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct.
All attacks were executed under the UCB primary planner, with separate planners used to select malicious categories.
Cell values represent the percentage of total category usage per planner. <a href="#footnote1" title="Footnote 1 ‣ Security Events Utility Exploit ‣ 4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
</figcaption>
