<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Challenge Progress and Overview</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our system evolved significantly throughout the Amazon Nova AI Challenge. In Tournament 1, we submitted an early version of RedTWIZ with four attack strategies and no planning component. It achieved a 10.8% static tool score and a final ASR of 27.83%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For Tournament 2, we expanded to seven attack strategies and deployed our attack-level adaptive planner. These upgrades improved the static score to 28.7% and raised our final ASR to 34.93%, the second-highest among all teams.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Tournament 3, we focused more on refining existing attack strategies and introduced the category-level planner. The static score dropped to 7.8%, a result influenced by more resilient defender models. This reflects the iterative nature of the challenge: defenders adapt to known attacks, forcing attackers to continuously innovate.
Our final result in Tournament 3, considering both static score and human annotations, reached 12.29%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This progression highlights RedTWIZ’s growing sophistication and the need for continuous innovation to remain effective against defenders with progressively stronger safety mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Tournament Performance</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reflects Tournament 2, as it was our best-performing iteration and better represents the overall performance of our system.
Table <a href="#S6.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ 6.1 Tournament Performance ‣ 6 Challenge Progress and Overview ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> summarizes the performance of our system against five anonymized defender models, reporting both the number of attacks issued per strategy and the resulting attack success rates as measured by Amazon’s annotation guidelines (for additional details, see Section <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Tournament 2 Breakdown: Static Tool vs. Human Annotations ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>). It is important to note that during this phase, we introduced the PAIR attack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, while MRT-Ferret (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 MRT-Ferret ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>) was still under development and was only deployed in Tournament 3.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The system leveraged the UCB planner to dynamically allocate attack types based on observed defender behavior. At this stage of the tournament, only attack-type planning was active, as the malicious category planner was introduced in Tournament 3. As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ 6.1 Tournament Performance ‣ 6 Challenge Progress and Overview ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, Utility Coding Exploit and Code Translation were the most frequently selected strategies. Utility Coding Exploit accounted for the majority of attacks against Defender 1, suggesting a degree of overfitting to the utility dataset, which likely made our attacks more difficult for the defender model to flag. Similarly, Code Translation demonstrated strong generalization, yielding particularly high ASR values for Defender 4 (76.7%) and Defender 3 (63%), suggesting that these models were more vulnerable when challenged with translating code from unfamiliar programming languages to the target Python language.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, strategies such as Security Events Utility Exploit and PAIR were selected less frequently, reflecting their lower performance across the board. Red-DAT, however, demonstrated strong potential in specific contexts: it was entirely excluded from some defender allocations due to underperformance in early stages, yet yielded a remarkably high 66.7% ASR against Defender 4.
This selective deployment underscores the planner’s ability to recognize and prioritize strategies that align well with a defender’s specific weaknesses.
RedTreez remained largely underutilized, likely because its distinctive tone and roleplay-driven style diverged from conventional utility prompt formats, making it potentially less effective and out-of-distribution relative to the defender models’ training data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the system achieved its highest success rate against Defender 4 (66%) and encountered the greatest resistance from Defender 5 (15.5%). This disparity illustrates the varied resilience of defender models and highlights the importance of strategic adaptation. The attack planner successfully shifted focus toward high-impact strategies when evidence of defender weakness emerged, allowing the system to maximize effectiveness within a limited budget of conversations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T15" class="ltx_table">
