</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Jailbreaking Specialized Defenders vs. General Purpose LLMs</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To contextualize performance in the Amazon Nova AI Challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahai et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we evaluated our system against three general-purpose LLMs: LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct, Claude 3.5 Sonnet v2, and Amazon Nova Pro, using the same controlled tournament simulation setup and our Jailbreak Judges (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
As previously shown in Table <a href="#S5.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 5.3 Cross-Model Generalization of Planner Effectiveness ‣ 5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, the evaluated models responded differently to targeted multi-turn adversarial prompts, demonstrating that general-purpose LLMs may still present openings for jailbreak strategies in controlled settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, the average ASR against the official Tournament 2 defenders was 34.93%, while the average ASR for Tournament 3 was 12.29%, accounting for the static code analysis and human annotations. While these results are not strictly comparable due to differences in evaluation methodology, they offer a useful approximation: general-purpose LLMs appear substantially more vulnerable than specialized defenders optimized for safety. Additional analysis in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Defenders’ Behavior Analysis ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a> shows that defender models often preemptively refused even benign prompts, indicating a high degree of strictness in response behavior. This underscores the importance of targeted alignment strategies when developing systems that aim to be robust against jailbreak attempts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Future Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on our current work, we are actively exploring several directions to improve RedTWIZ:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreak Judges.</span>
Combining the various Jailbreak Judges presented in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 RS1 Automatic LLM Jailbreak Assessment ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> into an Ensemble of Judges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> is expected to improve the evaluation accuracy and more precisely exploit weaknesses of the defender models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Strategies.</span>
Tournament results show that defenders tend to reject out-of-distribution prompts. Aligning attacks more closely with the utility dataset, inspired by principles from utility poisoning techniques (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Utility Poisoning Attacks ‣ 4 RS2 Diverse Multi-turn Attack-Suite ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), represents a promising direction to increase attack effectiveness.
The strong performance of original and previously unseen attacks further motivates the design of new strategies that escalate malicious intent while avoiding detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russinovich et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, for instance through self-reflection and iterative reformulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Categories and Planning.</span>
Given the success of attack category planning (Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Optimizing Malicious Category Allocation ‣ 5 RS3 Hierarchical RL-based Attack Planner ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>), refining the category set by removing underperforming categories, introducing new ones, and subdividing effective categories offers a path to more precisely target specific vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Ethics and Responsible Disclosure</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All evaluations presented in this work were conducted as part of a sanctioned red-teaming competition organized under the Amazon Nova AI Challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahai et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, where models were intentionally exposed to adversarial inputs within a red-teaming setup.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We coordinated with competition organizers to ensure vulnerabilities discovered through our system were responsibly disclosed. No functional malicious code was executed or retained. All simulated attacks and jailbreak prompts were generated in non-production environments and used solely to assess model robustness for safety improvement. Our work aligns with red-teaming guidelines outlined in the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and MLCommons.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All models, benchmarks, and tools referenced in this paper were used or cited in accordance with responsible usage and disclosure practices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Conclusions and Implications</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work introduces <span id="S9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Red<span id="S9.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">TWIZ</span></span>, a framework for automated red teaming of LLMs in multi-turn settings for AI-assisted software development. Our system combines automatic jailbreak assessment through various judge models, a diverse attack suite, and a hierarchical reinforcement learning-based planner that optimizes both attack strategy and malicious categories selection. By adapting to model-specific weaknesses and leveraging structured feedback, our framework enables scalable and effective adversarial testing of various models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Empirical evaluations across multiple defender models and challenge settings demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach. Notably, our system elicited unsafe completions across various attack vectors, especially with the introduction of novel and adaptive attack strategies such as Utility Poisoning, Coding Attacks, MRT-Ferret, RedTreez, and Red-DAT.
Moreover, the hierarchical planner proved critical in efficiently allocating attack resources, balancing exploration and exploitation across a wide range of adversarial techniques.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Red<span id="S9.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">TWIZ</span></span> contributes to the advancement of LLM safety by providing a practical framework for evaluating vulnerabilities through simulation, enabling more resilient model design. Insights from this system are used to strengthen model defenses, guide safety-focused deployment practices, and adversarial mitigation strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
