</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Explanation.</span> The <span id="A3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ExecuteAttack</span> procedure governs the full RedTreez attack cycle by iterating through alternating A (attack) and D (defense) nodes. At each A node, a set of candidate nodes is retrieved from the tree. If none exist, a new one is randomly generated using <span id="A3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CreateRandomANode</span>. Otherwise, one is selected via <span id="A3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SelectBestANode</span>, which applies the current mode’s selection strategy: <span id="A3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">population</span>, <span id="A3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">coverage</span>, or <span id="A3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exploitation</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The prompt is constructed using <span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CreatePrompt</span>, which combines a turn-specific guiding prompt (see Appendix <a href="#A8.SS7" title="H.7 RedTreez ‣ Appendix H System prompts ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.7</span></a>) with the current conversation history and the selected node’s strategies. Each guiding prompt is a string containing placeholders such as <span id="A3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{goal}</span>, <span id="A3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{history}</span>, and <span id="A3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{extra_info}</span>, where the latter is used to inject the list of selected strategies with a sentence like <span id="A3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Use these strategies: </span>{<span id="A3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">…</span>}<span id="A3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">”</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After querying the target model, the defense phase processes the model’s response. It is classified using <span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ClassifyResponse</span>, and if a jailbreak is detected via <span id="A3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">IsJailbreak</span>, the corresponding attack path is marked successful in the tree with <span id="A3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MarkSuccessPath</span>. The traversal then follows to a D node matching the response’s labels. If none exists, a new D node is created via <span id="A3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CreateChildDNode</span>. This process repeats until either a jailbreak is achieved or the maximum number of turns is reached.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This algorithm ensures that RedTreez can dynamically adjust its attack path based on the model’s reactions while leveraging structured, turn-specific prompts and adaptive node selection guided by the active exploration-exploitation strategy mode.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Red-DAT Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Red Teaming Prompt Setup and Dialogue Flow</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To simulate a natural dialogue structure aligned with typical LLM training, we begin each episode with a greeting from the defender model. Although the attacker conceptually initiates the dialogue, we simulate the defender speaking first to reflect typical LLM training data, in which conversations begin with a user message.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The original DAT greeting, <span id="A4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"Hello, how is your day?"</span>, often led to off-topic small talk, limiting the attacker’s ability to introduce the red-teaming objective. We replaced it with a more open-ended prompt, <span id="A4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"Hello! How can I assist you today?"</span>, which preserves natural flow while giving the attacker more flexibility to guide the conversation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This allows the attacker model more flexibility to introduce its objective while maintaining a natural conversational tone.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The system prompt used for the attacker LLM is in <a href="#A8.SS3" title="H.3 Red-DAT ‣ Appendix H System prompts ‣ RedTWIZ: Diverse LLM Red Teaming via Adaptive Attack Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.3</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Reinforcement Learning Training Details</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the RL stage, we follow the DAT training setup and use TD3+BC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fujimoto and Gu (2021)</a></cite>, an actor-critic algorithm effective in continuous action spaces. The offline replay buffer consists of 10,000 five-turn dialogues collected using the self-cloned planner, with Gaussian exploration noise <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,0.2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,0.2)</annotation></semantics></math> applied.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To stabilize training, we employ a residual planner architecture. Rather than updating the original planner <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> directly, we introduce a secondary planner <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\prime}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\prime}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> that learns a residual correction on top of the self-cloned output. The final action vector is computed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}=\pi_{\phi}(s_{t})+\pi^{\prime}_{\phi}\left(\frac{s_{t}-\mu}{\sigma+\epsilon}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}=\pi_{\phi}(s_{t})+\pi^{\prime}_{\phi}\left(\frac{s_{t}-\mu}{\sigma+\epsilon}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dialogue history embedding at turn <math id="A4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> are the empirical mean and standard deviation of the dialogue states. This formulation ensures that <math id="A4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\prime}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\prime}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> operates over normalized input and can stably refine the planner’s behavior without disrupting prior initialization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fujimoto and Gu (2021)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">At each conversation turn <math id="A4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the planner observes the current state <math id="A4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and selects an action vector <math id="A4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to steer the attacker LLM’s generation. The environment then returns a scalar reward <math id="A4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which reflects the effectiveness of the attack utterance for <math id="A4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Planner Attack Distribution Analysis</h2>

<figure id="A5.T19" class="ltx_table">
